findings.finding:
My current finding is that for each \(n\) the cyclicity condition \(B,K,N,K'\) admits **exactly one** positive incircle radius \(r\), and this root is extremely small – roughly \(r\approx10^{-(n-1)}\). Numerically,  

\[
r_{2}=1.5625\times10^{-2},\;
r_{3}=1.00\times10^{-3},\;
r_{4}=1.00\times10^{-4},\;
r_{5}=1.00\times10^{-5},\;
r_{6}=1.00\times10^{-6},
\]

found by solving \(\det\begin{pmatrix}B\\K\\K'\\N\end{pmatrix}=0\). For these radii the ratio  

\[
\frac{CT\cdot NB}{BT\cdot NE}
\]

grows without bound (e.g. \(a_{2}\approx2.05\times10^{3},\;a_{3}\approx8.19\times10^{3},\;a_{5}\approx3.28\times10^{4}\)). Hence the claim that a finite limit \(\alpha=p+\sqrt q\) exists is false; the ratio diverges as \(n\to\infty\). This disproves Solver 1’s assertion of a non‑trivial finite \(\alpha\).
